{
	title: 'hal serve log and --port',
	spec: 'hal serve writes a human-readable log to stdout, one line per event: "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS.mmm <text>" in local time, optionally followed by one-line ASON data. Other processes (terminal, hal -p) never write it. src/host/server-log.ts holds the API: log(text, data?), ms() ("(57 ms)" since process start) and cut(text) (one line, so the log line fits 100 columns). Startup: home, state dir, pid and arguments; the version from release.line (same as /version); session and open-tab counts; listening on the host socket (ms); web on http://127.0.0.1:<port>/ plus webUrl when set (ms). Session events, from host.broadcast: prompts (you:), assistant text (hal:, one line per text block), slash commands, questions (asks:), turn end with duration, and errors in full, never cut. Clients: connected (number, kind, address, user agent), hello (pid, version), left (connection time). Web login success and failure with address. /restart: its scope (all, host, both) and who asked (client or model) in which session. SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGUSR1 stops. Resource level changes (low, critical, recovered) with free disk, available memory and the host process memory. Every hour: connected clients, running turns, host memory, available memory, free disk. Silent otherwise. hal serve --port <n> listens only on that port and exits 1 with the reason when it cannot; without it, webPort and the next free port through 9100 as before.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Session events come from host.broadcast, so turn code is untouched. Assistant text is buffered per session until another event of that session, then written once (at most 200 chars kept). clients.fromRequest now trusts X-Forwarded-For only from a loopback connection (behind Caddy); otherwise the socket address, since the header could be forged. Verified: real hal serve on a scratch home with a hal/tool -p client, a busy --port exiting 1, SIGTERM and SIGUSR1 lines.',
